Report 25-26.pdf","committee":"Governance & Audit & Standards Committee","date":"2026-06-25","category":"treasury","fy":"2025/26"}],"calendar":{"upcoming":[{"date":"2026-09-21","committee":"Cabinet Member for Community Wellbeing, Health & Care","time":"5.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.portsmouth.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-22","committee":"Cabinet","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.portsmouth.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-23","committee":"Cabinet Member for Housing and Tackling Homelessness","time":"3.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.portsmouth.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-24","committee":"Cabinet Member for Environmental Services","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.portsmouth.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-05","committee":"Traffic, Environment & Community Safety Scrutiny Panel","time":"5.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.portsmouth.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-09","committee":"Cabinet Member - The Leader (Culture, Regeneration and Economic Development Portfolio)","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.portsmouth.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-13","committee":"Full Council","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.portsmouth.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-14","committee":"Cabinet Member for Central Services","time":"1.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.portsmouth.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-09-07","committee":"Economic Development, Culture and Leisure Scrutiny Panel","time":"1.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.portsmouth.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-11","committee":"Health Overview & Scrutiny Panel","time":"1.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.portsmouth.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-16","committee":"Education, Children & Young People Scrutiny Panel","time":"5.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.portsmouth.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-16","committee":"Governance & Audit & Standards Committee","time":"5.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.portsmouth.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-17","committee":"Cabinet Member for Transport","time":"4.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.portsmouth.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Portsmouth faces three-year audit backlog and may not clear accounts until 2028","lead":"Portsmouth's auditors have warned that the council will not achieve a fully unqualified audit opinion until after 2027/28, leaving its financial accounts lacking full independent verification for a third consecutive year. The external auditor, EY, issued a qualified opinion for 2024/25 because prior-year disclaimers had prevented sufficient audit evidence being gathered over major assets and reserves. During last year's audit, EY discovered a \u00a330m error in how properties were valued in 2023/24, now flagged as a significant risk. Before rebuilding assurance can begin, the council must complete a comprehensive reconciliation of reserves across all disclaimed years. The timeline for recovery extends beyond the current financial year because properties are revalued on a five-year rolling programme, with the next full cycle not completing until 2027/28. EY says it may not move beyond a qualified opinion until after that date.","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"PCC Audit Plan Addendum 2025-26","paper_url":"https://portsmouth.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s64232/64232.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-22","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Revenue Budget Monitoring 2026/2027 (First Quarter) to end June 2026","paper_url":"https://portsmouth.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s64396/64396.pdf","headline":"Treasury management underspend of \u00a3747,700 from higher interest rates, but council flags budget as volatile and rate-dependent.","detail":"The council has benefited from 'sustained higher interest rates' providing increased returns on short-term cash holdings. However, the paper explicitly notes the Treasury budget is 'potentially a very volatile budget particularly in the current economic climate and is extremely susceptible to both changes in interest rates.' This is clearly flagged as a one-off benefit.","category":"income","matters":"Shows the council is not in complete freefall (interest rates are helping), but this benefit is temporary. If rates fall, this income disappears and the underlying \u00a37.5m overspend becomes fully visible.","figures":"\u00a3747,700 underspend","quote":"The forecast underspend is largely because of the sustained higher interest rates which has provided the Council with increased levels of interest on its short-term cash flows.","material":false},{"iso":"2026-09-22","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Revenue Budget Monitoring 2026/2027 (First Quarter) to end June 2026","paper_url":"https://portsmouth.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s64396/64396.pdf","headline":"Deputy Chief Executive flags 'longer term uncertainty relating to further increases in demand' and warns council's savings requirements may need to rise.","detail":"The finance director notes that demand for services continues to grow and has not stabilized since the budget was approved in February. The recommendation to directors is to 'eliminate deficits in the medium term,' indicating the current trajectory is unsustainable. This is a direct signal from financial leadership that the council's position needs structural improvement.","category":"demand","matters":"Signal from the section 151 officer that current forecast is not sustainable and further budget gaps are likely unless demand growth can be arrested. Indicates future savings plans will need to be significantly larger than currently planned.","figures":"Homelessness demand +20% (551 vs 459 households); adult care volumes and costs rising; children's placements \u00a30.86m overspend","quote":"Whilst the approved Budget 2026/27 originally sought to remedy unavoidable budget pressures, since the budget was approved in February, demand for services continues to place pressure or exacerbate known pressures. Consequently, there remains longer term uncertainty relating to further increases in demand.","material":false},{"iso":"2026-09-22","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Revenue Budget Monitoring 2026/2027 (First Quarter) to end June 2026","paper_url":"https://portsmouth.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s64396/64396.pdf","headline":"Portfolio overspends of \u00a37.5m reduced to reported \u00a30.66m through contingency (\u00a33.8m) and reserve transfers (\u00a33.0m), raising sustainability questions.","detail":"Raw portfolio-level overspends total \u00a37.5m. Of this, \u00a33.8m is covered by drawing down Corporate Contingency (treating homelessness, crematorium maintenance, etc. as 'windfall costs'), and \u00a33.0m by planned reserve transfers. This leaves \u00a30.66m reported. Contingency is finite and reserves are being depleted, so this approach is not indefinitely sustainable.","category":"overspend","matters":"Lenders need to see the true operating position. The use of contingency and reserves to mask underlying overspends signals that the council is not genuinely balanced, and future fiscal pressures could emerge when these tools are exhausted.","figures":"\u00a37.5m gross portfolio overspend; \u00a33.8m contingency release; \u00a33.0m reserve transfers; \u00a30.66m net reported","quote":"After releases from Contingency (to fund Windfall Costs of \u00a33,847,800) and transfers to and from Reserves, the Council is forecasting an overall overspend of \u00a3658,500.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-22","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Revenue Budget Monitoring 2026/2027 (First Quarter) to end June 2026","paper_url":"https://portsmouth.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s64396/64396.pdf","headline":"Adult and children's social care costs rising due to volume growth and unit cost inflation: \u00a31.3m adult overspend, \u00a30.86m children's placements, \u00a30.46m transport.","detail":"Adult care driven by 'increase in client volumes and average package costs' (\u00a30.9m), staffing/agency inflation (\u00a30.2m), and lost income (\u00a30.26m). Children's services shows sustained cost pressures on care placements and school transport. These are not one-off items but reflect demographic and supply-side pressures.","category":"demand","matters":"Aging population plus supplier cost inflation create ongoing budget pressure. Both volumes and unit costs rising simultaneously means this will persist unless service delivery model changes.","figures":"Adult care \u00a31.3m overspend; children's placements \u00a30.86m overspend, transport \u00a30.46m overspend","quote":"The overspend is primarily due to higher than anticipated care package costs for Adult Care & Support clients within external Residential, Nursing and Domiciliary care placements, due to both an increase in client volumes and average package costs (\u00a30.9m).","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-22","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Revenue Budget Monitoring 2026/2027 (First Quarter) to end June 2026","paper_url":"https://portsmouth.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s64396/64396.pdf","headline":"Temporary accommodation demand up 20% in a year (551 households vs 459), driving \u00a33.2m overspend.","detail":"This is the single largest overspend. The council notes insufficient social housing provision to meet demand and has a plan to expand temporary accommodation portfolio, but current demand is far outpacing supply. The paper treats this as a 'windfall cost' but it is clearly structural.","category":"demand","matters":"Structural demand growth in homelessness is not cyclical and will persist unless the supply side catches up. This will continue to put pressure on the council's revenue budget and capital plan.","figures":"551 households at Q1 2026/27 vs 459 at Q1 2025/26 (20% increase); \u00a33.2m cost overspend","quote":"At the end of Quarter 1 2026/27, 551 households were in temporary accommodation, compared with 459 at the end of Quarter 1 2025/26.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-16"},"name":"Portsmouth UA","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-19","since_iso":"2026-08-20","distress":false,"headline":"Council deferring borrowing due to improved Q1 cash position and elevated PWLB rates","lead":"The material variance between approved borrowing (\u00a31,084m) and actual (\u00a3712m) indicates either conservative forecasting or unexpectedly strong cash generation in Q1. When cash reserves reduce, the council will need to borrow at current elevated rates.\u2026","also":["11 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Cabinet Member for Community Wellbeing, Health & Care on 2026-09-21","n_findings":12,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Qtr 1 Treasury Management Review 26-27 with Appendix A Final","top_paper_url":"https://portsmouth.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s64260/64260.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":122,"news_drafts_total":1}}